  "nl_text": "The database schema named rschema_0095 is used to store information about customers, employees, sales opportunities, communication records, and service requests. The schema consists of five tables: Customer, Employee, Sales_Opportunity, Communication_Record, and Service_Request.\nThe Customer table contains information about customers, including their address, company name, customer ID, email, gender, name, and phone number. The Customer ID is the primary key, and it is a unique identifier for each customer.\nThe Employee table contains information about employees, including their department, email, employee ID, gender, level, name, phone number, and position. The Employee ID is the primary key, and it is a unique identifier for each employee.\nThe Sales_Opportunity table contains information about sales opportunities, including the actual transaction period, creation time, customer ID, employee ID, estimated amount, estimated transaction date, opportunity name, opportunity stage, status, and primary key. The primary key is a composite key consisting of the customer ID, employee ID, and creation time. The foreign keys reference the Customer and Employee tables.\nThe Communication_Record table contains information about communication records, including communication content, communication method, communication result, communication stage, creation time, customer ID, employee ID, and primary key. The primary key is a composite key consisting of the customer ID, employee ID, and creation time. The foreign keys reference the Customer and Employee tables.\nThe Service_Request table contains information about service requests, including creation time, customer ID, employee ID, request description, request type, solution, and status. The primary key is a composite key consisting of the customer ID, employee ID, and creation time. The foreign keys reference the Customer and Employee tables.\nIn summary, the rschema_0095 database schema is used to store information about customers, employees, sales opportunities, communication records, and service requests. Each table has its own set of columns, and the primary keys and foreign keys are used to establish relationships between the tables.",
  "gold_question": "Customer Relationship Management (CRM) systems are tools used by companies to manage interactions with customers, improve customer satisfaction and loyalty, and optimize sales and marketing processes. With increasing competition and changes in the market environment, many companies have realized that maintaining and improving relationships with customers is essential for long-term success. Through efficient customer data management, accurate market segmentation, timely customer follow-up, and personalized services, companies can increase customer stickiness, improve sales and service quality, and thus achieve higher market share and customer satisfaction.\nCRM systems help companies manage customers comprehensively by integrating basic customer information, communication records, sales opportunities, service requests, etc. This system not only improves the work efficiency of marketing and sales teams, but also helps customer service departments provide more personalized services, thereby forming healthy customer relationships.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0095.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0095\nCREATE TABLE `Customer` (\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `company_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`customer_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Employee` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `gender` char(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `department` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `level` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Sales_Opportunity` (\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `creation_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `opportunity_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `opportunity_stage` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `estimated_amount` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `estimated_transaction_date` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`customer_id`, `employee_id`, `creation_time`),\n  CONSTRAINT `sales_opportunity_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `Customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `sales_opportunity_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `Employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Communication_Record` (\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `creation_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `communication_content` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `communication_method` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `communication_result` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `communication_stage` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`customer_id`, `employee_id`, `creation_time`),\n  CONSTRAINT `communication_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `Customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `communication_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `Employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Service_Request` (\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `creation_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `request_description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `request_type` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `solution` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`customer_id`, `employee_id`, `creation_time`),\n  CONSTRAINT `service_request_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `Customer` (`customer_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `service_request_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`employee_id`) REFERENCES `Employee` (`employee_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
